=== Psalm 23
--- Psalm 23 [title]
overline: The Book of Psalms
heading: Psalm 23
sub: The LORD is my shepherd
refs: Psalm 23:1

--- The Lord is my shepherd
kicker: Psalm 23 · The good shepherd
big: Psalm 23:1
p: **Psalm 23** is perhaps the second most famous passage of scripture, behind **John 3:16**, and like it, contains the gospel promise, the good news of the Lord towards believers.

--- By faith in the Good Shepherd, alone
kicker: Psalm 23 · The good shepherd
p: The world knows the Psalm because it has become the standard go to, in fiction and in real life, recited at funerals by priests and reverends, in every religion that claims the name of Christ. Sadly, for most, the departed have no idea that the promises are not automatic, and aren't achieved by their works, their character or how "good" they are. They are by faith in the Good Shepherd, alone.
p: Of course, Jesus is that shepherd.
big: John 10:11 | mark: I am the good shepherd

--- I shall not want
kicker: Psalm 23 · I shall not want
p: He gave His life that we may be forgiven, and rose again that we may have eternal life. He did this for those that follow Him, and who have been entrusted to Him by the Father. Believers are His flock.
p: And because He is our shepherd, we shall not want.
p: What an amazing promise, which God Who cannot lie has given us. We shall not want. He provides all things:
big: Philippians 4:19 | mark: my God shall supply all your need

--- All things that pertain unto life
kicker: Psalm 23 · I shall not want
p: Not riches as this world judges, necessarily, but all our needs are met by Him by Christ Jesus.
big: 2 Peter 1:3 | mark: all things that pertain unto life and godliness
p: You may feel financially burdened, or pressed by health concerns. You might be struggling in your faith, to live by trust in God to deliver. But ultimately, the truth is that all we need, everything that pertains to life and godliness, is given freely, that we may have life and more abundantly in His name.

--- Beside the still waters
kicker: Psalm 23 · Green pastures
big: Psalm 23:2
p: The green pastures and still waters are generally equated to Heaven in the way the world uses **Psalm 23**, and certainly, what little we know of Paradise is the peace of being in the presence of the Lord. But this stillness is a promise of this life, for a believer.
big: Philippians 4:7 | mark: the peace of God, which passeth all understanding

--- All things work together for good
kicker: Psalm 23 · Green pastures
p: Our burdens aren't removed, but the yoke of carrying them is made light by His grace. Because in the end, whatever we are going through will be for our good.
big: Romans 8:28

--- He restoreth my soul
kicker: Psalm 23 · For his name's sake
big: Psalm 23:3 | mark: He restoreth my soul
p: We are renewed in Him, and regenerated by the Spirit in faith, our soul restored. We are led in His ways, and shown the truth from His word, for His name's sake. In Jesus we are made whole, and by His power revealed in us, by His word, we are shown the deeper things of God.

--- The valley of the shadow of death
kicker: Psalm 23 · I will fear no evil
big: Psalm 23:4 | mark: the valley of the shadow of death; for thou art with me
p: This allegory is considered death itself by the secular world, but note the imagery of a shadow. The Bible says a shadow is not the very image of the things that cast it.
p: We may be in a low point in our lives, a valley, and maybe even feel the shadow of death on our flesh as we endure this season in our lives, but the strength to fear no evil, to glory in our tribulation, comes from knowing the good Shepherd is with us. His rod and staff, our comfort.

--- Thy rod and thy staff
kicker: Psalm 23 · I will fear no evil
p: The rod of correction, the staff that guides us as we go astray. His protection, our safety. Knowing He laid down His life for us. That He will seek and save His lost sheep, and bring us home by His power.
p: What the unsaved world uses as a false comfort in death should be a comfort in this life for saved believers.

--- A table before me
kicker: Psalm 23 · My cup runneth over
big: Psalm 23:5 | mark: Thou preparest a table before me in the presence of mine enemies; my cup runneth over
p: The table He has set for us, the bounty of His blessings, the deliverance from those who seek to destroy us, that they may know we fear the Lord, and glorify Him, is in this life. In the presence of our enemies.
p: Yes, there is the idea of a feast set for us in the end times prophecy, a wedding feast for the Bridegroom. Served by Christ Jesus:

--- A feast of fat things
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 23:5
big: Isaiah 25:6 | mark: a feast of fat things
big: Luke 12:37 | mark: will come forth and serve them
p: ...but the primary application in **Psalm 23** is the peace found in this life. At this time. Our cup runneth over with His grace, even now. The table has been set in Heaven, but the bounty we are given starts the moment you call upon His name in faith.

--- Goodness and mercy
kicker: Psalm 23 · All the days of my life
p: The final verse places this context firmly in the "now" of the believer:
big: Psalm 23:6 | mark: all the days of my life
p: Goodness and mercy follow us, today. We can rest assured that He has delivered us, and be blessed in this life, knowing we have eternity with Him in the next.
p: We shall fear no evil, because we have eternal life and shall never perish. We will dwell in the house of the Lord forever, so rejoice even in perils and trials. Rest in the green pastures by the still waters. We have a good Shepherd Who loves us. Amen

=== Psalm 24
--- Psalm 24 [title]
overline: The Book of Psalms
heading: Psalm 24
sub: Who shall ascend? • The King of glory
refs: Psalm 24:1

--- The earth is the Lord's
kicker: Psalm 24 · The Creator
scripture: Psalm 24:1-2 | mark: The earth is the LORD’s, and the fulness thereof
p: **Psalm 24** is a meditation on the truth of the Christ, the Lord, King of Hosts. Creator of all things, and the truth of the way of righteousness for those that trust in Him.
p: The gospel message of the Psalms, sung in the Temple, performed by the chief musicians and the congregation is the inspired doctrines of God, and the focus is entirely Jesus.
p: They may have looked through a glass darkly, but the concepts of Christ are hammered home, in verse after verse, chorus and stanzas of praise for the name of God, the Holy One, the Lord.

--- Who shall ascend?
kicker: Psalm 24 · The hill of the Lord
scripture: Psalm 24:3-4 | mark: Who shall ascend into the hill of the LORD?; He that hath clean hands, and a pure heart
p: Here in the 24th Psalm, David asks *Who shall ascend into the hill of the LORD? or who shall stand in his holy place?*
p: Those with clean hands and pure hearts, who hasn't put their trust in vanity or lying lips, but who trusted the God of their salvation.

--- Righteousness from the God of his salvation
kicker: Psalm 24 · Imputed righteousness
big: Psalm 24:5 | mark: righteousness from the God of his salvation
p: We are made clean of our sins, our hearts purified by the imputed righteousness of God. We receive these blessings by grace through faith, and access to the Kingdom, not by our own righteousness but by submitting to the righteousness that is God's.

--- Righteousness without works
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 24:5
scripture: Romans 4:5-8 | mark: unto whom God imputeth righteousness without works

--- Made the righteousness of God in him
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 24:5
p: There are false religions that deny the imputation of righteousness by faith in Christ. They say that imputation is not taught in scripture. This is a lie.
big: 2 Corinthians 5:21 | mark: that we might be made the righteousness of God in him

--- Their own righteousness
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 24:4–5
p: Our righteousness is as a filthy rag, our works could never justify us before a holy and perfect God. But these men believing vanity, and swearing deceitfully, teach that man needs to establish their own righteousness through works of the law, and many are misled by this lie.
scripture: Romans 10:2-4 | mark: going about to establish their own righteousness

--- The Council of Trent
kicker: Application • Imputed righteousness
p: Roman Catholicism rejects the idea that we are made righteous by receiving righteousness from the God of our salvation. The Council of Trent (Canon 11 on Justification) anathematized the idea that people are justified “by the sole imputation of the justice of Christ, or by the sole remission of sins” and that righteousness is "infused" by works, sacraments and "grace transforming a person," so that their own righteousness is established, rather than submitting to the righteousness of God.

--- Them that seek him
kicker: Psalm 24 · The generation
big: Psalm 24:6 | mark: This is the generation of them that seek him
p: But let God be true, and every man a liar. The generation of them that seek His face shall not be ashamed. All who come to the saving knowledge of Christ are made pure, in Him. Righteous, in Him.
scripture: Titus 3:5-7 | mark: Not by works of righteousness which we have done

--- Remembered no more
kicker: Application • Imputed righteousness
p: The truth of imputed righteousness of God being given the believer is found throughout scripture. And thank God for His grace and mercy. That our sins are washed clean, justified by the blood, and remembered no more.

--- Who is this King of glory?
kicker: Psalm 24 · The King of glory
scripture: Psalm 24:7-10 | mark: Who is this King of glory?
p: The final verses of **Psalm 24** are pure prophecy, the adoration and glory of the King.
p: The opening of the gates to receive Him. The Lord strong and mighty. Who is the King of glory? We know His name is Jesus. King of kings, Lord of Hosts. The everlasting doors of the Kingdom of God open, and receive Him. All glory is given to Him. The Creator Who is blessed forever, amen.

=== Psalm 25
--- Psalm 25 [title]
overline: The Book of Psalms
heading: Psalm 25
sub: Unto thee, O LORD, do I lift up my soul
refs: Psalm 25:1

--- Let none that wait on thee be ashamed
kicker: Psalm 25 · I lift up my soul
scripture: Psalm 25:1-3 | mark: Unto thee, O LORD, do I lift up my soul; let none that wait on thee be ashamed
p: The powerful prayer of **Psalm 25** is a model for all that trust in the Lord, and a doctrinally-rich reminder of why we should. That we who wait on the Lord will not be ashamed.
p: Verse after verse, the psalmist has us call upon the name of the Lord, as we read or sing through the Psalm. And verse after verse we are reminded of His mercy and faithfulness.
p: We lift up our souls, every fiber of our being, unto the Lord, and He delivers us from our enemies. Those that trust in Him are never ashamed, but those that reject His truth are.

--- Shew me thy ways
kicker: Psalm 25 · Teach me thy paths
scripture: Psalm 25:4-5 | mark: Shew me thy ways, O LORD; teach me thy paths
p: We are shown His ways, taught His statutes, walk His paths, and we are preserved in this life, and established in the next.

--- Remember not the sins of my youth
kicker: Psalm 25 · Thy tender mercies
scripture: Psalm 25:6-7 | mark: Remember not the sins of my youth
p: The meek and his seed shall inherit the earth, and in meekness we humbly admit our sins and ask for God's mercy, to forget them. His lovingkindness is established from old, and the God of our salvation keeps us by His power, unto redemption. What an amazing God we have. How can we not serve Him in truth. Truth of Who He is, who we are, and the reconciliation we have with and in Him, despite our sins.

--- He will teach sinners in the way
kicker: Psalm 25 · The meek
scripture: Psalm 25:8-11 | mark: therefore will he teach sinners in the way; the meek will he teach his way
p: The Lord teaches sinners like us in His way, and the meek share this truth with the world.

--- The secret of the Lord
kicker: Psalm 25 · His covenant
scripture: Psalm 25:12-14 | mark: The secret of the LORD is with them that fear him
p: His secret, His covenant, is shown to us. And that good news is spread abroad by those that fear Him, that others may know the mystery of the gospel, and be saved.

--- Out of the net
kicker: Psalm 25 · Mine eyes are ever toward the Lord
big: Psalm 25:15 | mark: he shall pluck my feet out of the net
big: Proverbs 3:6
big: Galatians 5:16 | mark: Walk in the Spirit
p: Looking to Jesus the author and finisher of our salvation, we are lifted from the nets, the snares of sin set for us by the wicked. Walking in the Spirit. Acknowledging the ways of the Lord.

--- Bring thou me out of my distresses
kicker: Psalm 25 · Have mercy upon me
scripture: Psalm 25:16-17 | mark: O bring thou me out of my distresses
p: With importunity and persistent prayer, we remind Him of the goodness He has shown us, and ourselves of the grace we have found in Him. Our troubles become lighter, as His yoke is easy. He brings us out of our distress.

--- Forgive all my sins
kicker: Psalm 25 · Let me not be ashamed
scripture: Psalm 25:18-22 | mark: forgive all my sins; Redeem Israel, O God
p: The pain we feel, He understands. The affliction that besets us is often due to sin. Seeking forgiveness, because our troubles may be chastening by a loving Father. Getting right so we may be lifted up by Him. Delivered, because we put our trust in Him. Never ashamed, because His word is true. Waiting on the day of the Lord, and the redemption of all Israel. All His people who trusted in the Lord. Amen

--- An alphabetic acrostic
kicker: Psalm 25 · A note on the Hebrew
p: In the original Hebrew, David used the format of an "alphabetic acrostic psalm," where stanzas begin with consecutive letters, to act as a mnemonic device, and form of poetry, that David uses multiple times in Psalms.
p: This stylized form of writing is "lost" in translation, some textual critics may say, but the strained gnat of it all makes them miss the power of what we have in English.
p: The repeated calls to trust the Lord, seek forgiveness, humbly petition and wait with patience is all beautifully expressed in Psalm 25 as we read it here. The prose of the Authorized English loses nothing to the power of God's word.
p: So, while it is interesting to note the format, and discuss the ways it was used (here, imperfectly with missing and skipped letters) it isn't necessary for the truth to prick our hearts and give us comfort.

--- The broken acrostic
kicker: Psalm 25 · A note on the Hebrew
p: However, recognizing **Psalm 25** as an attempted alphabetic acrostic in the original Hebrew, intended to run through the 22 letters of the alphabet, does give us the opportunity to see something of ourselves in it.
p: In this Psalm, the alphabetic pattern is incomplete, the letter *vav* is missing, *resh* appears twice, and the final verse falls outside the sequence. The broken acrostic still carries the psalm’s prayer, and focuses on the Lord's mercy and power.
p: Could the broken format look to the broken-hearted sinner, imperfectly calling upon a perfect God? Could it be that by breaking the expected format, David shows the same brokenness of spirit we have in this life as we wait upon the redemption of Christ in the end?
p: I don't know. But it sounds good. Selah.

